<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Fiume</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1924 CE · capital Fiume</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Internationally recognised independent city-state around the port of Fiume, created to settle the dispute between Italy and Yugoslavia and annexed by Italy in 1924.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rapallo-1920">Treaty of Rapallo</a>, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a> in November 1920, created the Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> as a compromise over the disputed port. After Italian forces ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">D'Annunzio</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-regency-of-carnaro">Italian Regency of Carnaro</a> in January 1921, elections in April 1921 were won by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autonomist-party-fiume">Autonomist Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riccardo-zanella">Riccardo Zanella</a>, who favoured independence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riccardo-zanella">Zanella</a>'s government struggled with economic collapse, as the port had lost its Hungarian hinterland, and with violent opposition from Italian nationalists and Fascists. In March 1922 a Fascist coup drove Zanella into exile, and Italian military authorities effectively took over. The population was mainly Italian, Croatian and Hungarian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rome">Treaty of Rome</a> in January 1924, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> partitioned the territory: Italy annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a>, while Yugoslavia received the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susak">Sušak</a> district. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city became Rijeka in Yugoslavia, and most Italians left.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-fiume.jpg" alt="Free State of Fiume" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_State_of_Fiume.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1924 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fiume</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Riccardo Zanella</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Croatian, Hungarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fiume, Stato libero di Fiume</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=free-state-of-fiume" aria-label="Free State of Fiume on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5023 578 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Fiume lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5343 834L5343 834M5343 834L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5343 834h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5343 834h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5343 834h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1922&amp;focus=free-state-of-fiume">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
